   "source": [
    "# Research Lab Notebook: Transformer Mechanics\n",
    "This notebook focuses on the **First Principles** of the Transformer architecture, simulating the "scientific rigor" required for the AI/ML Researcher role."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import torch\n",
    "import torch.nn as nn\n",
    "import torch.nn.functional as F\n",
    "import math\n",
    "\n",
    "def scaled_dot_product_attention(query, key, value, mask=None):\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    The core math of the Transformer.\n",
    "    Query: (Batch, Heads, Seq_Len, d_k)\n",
    "    \"\"\"\n",
    "    d_k = query.size(-1)\n",
    "    scores = torch.matmul(query, key.transpose(-2, -1)) / math.sqrt(d_k)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    if mask is not None:\n",
    "        scores = scores.masked_fill(mask == 0, -1e9)\n",
    "    \n",
    "    p_attn = F.softmax(scores, dim=-1)\n",
    "    return torch.matmul(p_attn, value), p_attn"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 🔬 Experiment Idea: Attention Visualization\n",
    "In research, you often need to visualize attention maps to understand why a model is failing on certain tasks."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Simulate some data\n",
    "batch, heads, seq_len, d_k = 1, 8, 10, 64\n",
    "q = torch.randn(batch, heads, seq_len, d_k)\n",
    "k = torch.randn(batch, heads, seq_len, d_k)\n",
    "v = torch.randn(batch, heads, seq_len, d_k)\n",
    "\n",
    "output, attn_map = scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v)\n",
    "print(f\"Output Shape: {output.shape}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Attention Map Shape: {attn_map.shape}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 🧪 Hypothetical Experiment: Testing RMSNorm\n",
    "Researchers must decide between normalization techniques. Let's implement a minimal RMSNorm to understand its efficiency."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "class RMSNorm(nn.Module):\n",
    "    def __init__(self, dim, eps=1e-6):\n",
    "        super().__init__()\n",
    "        self.eps = eps\n",
    "        self.weight = nn.Parameter(torch.ones(dim))\n",
    "\n",
    "    def _norm(self, x):\n",
    "        # The math: x / sqrt(mean(x^2) + eps)\n",
    "        return x * torch.rsqrt(x.pow(2).mean(-1, keepdim=True) + self.eps)\n",
    "\n",
    "    def forward(self, x):\n",
    "        return self._norm(x.float()).type_as(x) * self.weight"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Key Takeaways for the Research Interview:\n",
    "1. **Mathematical Foundations:** Be ready to derive the square-root scaling in attention (it prevents gradients from vanishing during softmax).\n",
    "2. **Implementation Cleanliness:** Notice the use of `.type_as(x)` in `RMSNorm`. A researcher must consider precision (FP16/FP32) during training.\n",
    "3. **Experimental Rigor:** When implementing a \"novel\" layer, always compare it against a standard baseline (like LayerNorm) on a small-scale task first."
   ]
  }
